Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a blend rolling on an edge (surface/restriction walking) stopped
where a step was too large (mitcad#121).

BRepBlend_SurfRstLineBuilder::InternalPerform: a section whose contacts lie
outside their domains and cannot be reframed on an arc (on the surface, on
the restriction, or where the ball leaves the restriction) ended the walk
at once (Blend_SamePoints). A step that is too large lands such sections:
on a long rounding the guide's maximal step reaches the other branch of
the solution (a ball on the far side of the restriction), whose contact
lies outside the face. As BRepBlend_Walking does for blends between two
faces, the step is now halved (Blend_StepTooLarge) while it is larger than
twice the guide's tolerance; only then does the walk stop there.

Test:
geometry/tests/port/p0015-rolling-walk-halves-a-step-off-the-far-branch.cpp
(ctest geometry.port.0015), test_rounding_rolls_round_a_wide_steps_rim: a
round step 0.5 high and 100 in radius on a plate, its foot rounded with
0.6, the ball rolling on the step's top rim all round: built, valid, the
exact volume, the step's side gone. The walk's steps of 4.5 to 9 degrees
start the solver, from the previous section, nearer the ball on the far
side of the rim (its centre 99.41 from the axis against 100.59), whose
plate contact under the step cannot be reframed. The second rounding of
geometry/tests/test_fillet_overflow.cpp's
test_second_rounding_rolls_on_the_first_ones_contact_line rolls inside
its rim, where such a start drifts towards the right ball, and so never
came here. test_rounding_rolls_round_a_narrow_steps_rim, the boundary: the
step 8.5 in radius, whose steps start by the right ball. It passes without
the patch: the test does not prove it (shown 2026-11-20 by
tools/occt-port-proof.py).
